<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="scopoletin biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: Enzyme(s) that catalyzes one or more of the key reactions in this pathway were predicted in Oryza sativa Japonica Group. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi_pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; |FRAME: SCOPOLETIN Scopoletin| (7-hydroxy-6-methoxy coumarin) is a hydroxycoumarin. It is found in a number of plant species including Arabidopsis, cassava, sweet potato, sunflower, cotton and tobacco |CITS: [19004461], [18547395]|. Scopoletin is thought to be involved in plant defense responses. In tobacco, accumulation of scopoletin was shown to strongly correlate with tobacco mosaic virus-induced localized acquired resistance |CITS: [12060240]|. In addition, |FRAME: SCOPOLETIN| serves as a precursor for the production of the siderophores |FRAME: CPD-21613| and |FRAME: CPD-21615 sideretin| |CITS: [29581584]|. Root exudation of phenylpropanoid-derived coumarins has been shown to be required for efficient iron uptake, especially under alkaline conditions. Mutations of the oxoglutarate-dependent oxidase |FRAME: AT3G13610|, involved in |FRAME: SCOPOLETIN| biosynthesis, and the ABC-type transporter PDR9, involved in coumarin export, result in severe symptoms of iron deficiency when iron is supplied in an insoluble form |CITS: [23735511][24015802][24246380][25058345]|. Scopoletin was indicated to have many pharmacological effects as well, such as antidepressant |CITS: [20599906]|, anti-arthritic |CITS: [21078410]|, anti-gastro-esophageal inflammatory |CITS: [21163341]|, potential in ameliorate insulin resistance |CITS: [20886413]|, and potential in treatment of hypertriglyceridemia |CITS: [17786283]|. In addition, it was also shown to have antitermite activity |CITS: [20563628]|. The biosynthesis pathway leading to scopoletin in plants is still largely unclear. Genetic and biochemical evidence in Arabidopsis supports that, in this plant, scopoletin is derived from feruloyl-CoA, not ferulate |CITS: [18547395]| as previously thought. A 2-oxoglutarate-dependent dioxygenase is responsible for the ortho-hydroxylation of feruloyl-CoA, an important branch point from lignin biosynthesis. Ortho-hydroxylation of the feruloyl-CoA aromatic ring is followed by trans-cis isomerization of the side chain, and subsequent lactonization to close the ring and yield scopoletin. The mechanism of the E-Z isomerization step in the biosynthesis of coumarins varies between genera or even species, for being either light-dependent photoisomerization or light-independent enzyme catalyzed isomerization |CITS: [19004461]|. It is not clear what mechanism is involved in the above depicted scopoletin biosynthesis pathway in Arabidopsis. In cassava, where scopoletin and its glycoside derivative appear to be involved in significant crop losses due to Post-harvest Physiological Deterioration (PPD), some detailed experiments performed with isotopic intermediates have shed light on the reactions comprising this |FRAME: PWY-7186 biosynthetic pathway| in |FRAME: TAX-3983| |CITS: [19004461]|.</Description>
